= 2nd National Congress of the Kuomintang

# reference: TBD

The establishment of the United Front with the CCP and the death of Dr. Sun Yat-sen in March of last year has left the party leadership in limbo. In November, allegations of Comintern infiltration in the Central Executive Committee by the Western Hills Group sparked disbelief and confusion among the rank-and-file, with calls for both the expulsion of the suspects and denunciation of the conservatives' accusations. Simple discipline has been unsuccessful in curbing the radicalizing sentiment throughout the party, encouraging the CEC to call for an unprecedented national congress, bringing all self-proclaimed Nationalists from across the nation to the city of Guangzhou to determine the future of the party.

- @congress1

@congress1
title: What of the Western Hills Group?
#on-arrival: kpd_party_leader = "Conciliators"; communist_coalition += 3; kpd_relation += 10*(1-dissent); left_strength += 5; left_dissent -= 8

The Western Hills Group have outrighted refused to participate what they perceive as a Bolshevik farce. While not unexpected, it is concerning, and sets a bad precedent for the future unity of the party.

- @congress2
- @congress3

@congress2
title: Can we convince them otherwise?
choose-if: (left_strength >= reformist_strength) 
unavailable-subtitle: They are unwilling to accept any compromise with leftists.

I see you cheating. I know I could write an entire event for this, but that's a lot of work, so I'm just going to recorrect you to the historical route.

- @congress3

@congress3
title: Forget about them, we'll hold it anyway.

The National Congress begins.
